# CLI and Tools Reference
{
    @meta {
        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    # VSCode Extension Commands
    {
        The SDOC extension registers eight commands, available from the Command Palette:

        {[.]
            - **SDOC: Open Preview** (`sdoc.preview`) -- opens a rendered preview of the current `.sdoc` file
            - **SDOC: Open Preview to the Side** (`sdoc.previewToSide`) -- opens the preview in a side pane
            - **SDOC: Export HTML** (`sdoc.exportHtml`) -- exports the current document as a standalone HTML file
            - **SDOC: Export PDF** (`sdoc.exportPdf`) -- exports the current document as an A4 PDF via headless Chrome
            - **SDOC: Open in Browser** (`sdoc.openInBrowser`) -- opens the current document in the system browser
            - **SDOC: Browse Documents** (`sdoc.browseDocs`) -- starts a local document server and opens the viewer in a browser
            - **SDOC: New Knowledge File** (`sdoc.newKnowledgeFile`) -- creates a new `.sdoc` file from a template in the selected folder
            - **SDOC: Generate About** (`sdoc.generateAbout`) -- generates an About section for the current document
        }

        The preview updates automatically when the source file changes or when a `sdoc.config.json` or `.css` file is saved.

        # Format Document
        {
            Use **Format Document** (Shift+Option+F on macOS, Shift+Alt+F on Windows/Linux) to auto-indent the current `.sdoc` file based on brace depth. The formatter respects VS Code's tab size and spaces/tabs preference. Code blocks are left untouched.
        }

        # Export HTML
        {
            Exports the current SDOC document as a self-contained `.html` file. Works from the editor or the preview panel. The exported file includes all styles and supports collapsible scope toggles in the browser.

            **Only `@reading-guide` of the signposts is kept by default.** An exported file is normally sent to a recipient who has already been asked to read it and will not edit it. `@about`, `@not-about` and `@related-resources` help someone decide whether to read, and `@editing-guide` is for editors, so all four are dropped. `@reading-guide` is written for the reader holding the copy, so it stays. Only signposts at the top level are dropped: a scope with a reserved id deeper down is exported as an ordinary section, and the command shows a warning for it. To choose otherwise, invoke the command programmatically with the renderer's options: `includeSignposts: true` keeps every signpost, `includeAbout: true` keeps `@about` as well as the default, and `signposts` keeps or drops single sections (see [Signposts in exports](api.sdoc#signposts-in-exports)):

            ```javascript
vscode.commands.executeCommand("sdoc.exportHtml", { includeSignposts: true });
vscode.commands.executeCommand("sdoc.exportHtml", { signposts: { "related-resources": true } });
            ```
        }

        # Export PDF
        {
            Exports the current SDOC document as an A4 PDF. Works from the editor or the preview panel. Requires Google Chrome or Chromium installed on the system (or set the `CHROME_PATH` environment variable). The PDF uses print-optimized styles with correct font sizes and preserved marker colors.

            Like Export HTML, only `@reading-guide` of the signposts is kept by default, and the command takes the same options:

            ```javascript
vscode.commands.executeCommand("sdoc.exportPdf", { includeSignposts: true });
            ```
        }

        # Open in Browser
        {
            Opens the current SDOC document in the system's default browser. Works from the editor or the preview panel. A temporary HTML file is created and opened. Like Export HTML, the output supports collapsible toggles and printing.
        }
    }

    # Build Document Tool
    {
        `tools/build-doc.js` exports SDOC files to HTML or PDF from the command line.

        ```bash
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc                   # PDF output (default)
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc -o output.pdf      # PDF with custom path
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc --html             # HTML output
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc --html -o out.html  # HTML with custom path
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc --include-signposts     # keep every signpost in the export
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc --signposts related-resources,-reading-guide  # pick single sections
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc --check            # validate only; exit 1 on any error
node tools/build-doc.js input.sdoc --fix              # put the signposts in order (rewrites the file)
        ```

        # Options
        {
            {[.]
                - `-o <path>` -- output file path (default: input name with `.pdf` or `.html` extension)
                - `--html` -- export as HTML instead of PDF
                - `--include-signposts` -- keep every signpost in the export. By default only `@reading-guide` is kept: an exported file is normally sent to a recipient who has already been asked to read it and will not edit it, so the discovery sections (`@about`, `@not-about`, `@related-resources`) and `@editing-guide` are noise for them. Only signposts at the top level are dropped: a scope with a reserved id deeper down is exported as an ordinary section, and a `Warning: line N:` message is printed for it
                - `--include-about` -- keep `@about` as well as the default
                - `--signposts <list>` -- keep or drop single signposts, applied after the two flags above. The list is comma-separated ids; an id is kept, or dropped when prefixed with `-`. The flag may be repeated. An unknown id is an error (exit 1)
                - `--check` -- validate the document: parse errors, signposts against their content models, broken references and links, and citations. Each finding is printed as `file:line: message`, or `file:line: warning: message` for a warning, such as a signpost out of order or an unused citation. The exit code is 1 when an error was found; warnings alone leave it at 0
                - `--fix` -- rewrite the `.sdoc` in place so `@meta` and the signposts are in their conventional order. It prints what it did, or why it could not do it safely; in that case it leaves the file unchanged and the exit code is 1. Combine it with `--check` to see what remains
                - With `--check` or `--fix`, nothing is exported, so neither needs Chrome. Add `-o` or `--html` to export as well, after the fix and the check
                - `--help` / `-h` -- show usage information and exit
            }
        }

        PDF export requires Google Chrome or Chromium. Set the `CHROME_PATH` environment variable to override auto-detection. The output uses A4 page size with print-optimized styles.

        The tool respects `sdoc.config.json` files in the directory hierarchy and per-file `@meta` style overrides, just like the VS Code extension.
    }

    # Build Slides Tool
    {
        `tools/build-slides.js` turns an SDOC file into a slide deck: HTML, PDF, or a
        PowerPoint file that Drive imports as a native Google Slides deck. Each top-level
        scope becomes one slide. See
        [Slide Authoring](slide-authoring.sdoc) for the deck format and the layouts.

        ```bash
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc                      # HTML output (default)
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc -o slides.html        # HTML with custom path
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pdf                 # PDF, one page per slide
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pptx                # PowerPoint / Google Slides
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --check               # report slides that overflow
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --theme themes/mine   # use a custom theme
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --fit cover           # off-ratio window behaviour
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pdf --with-optional # keep the optional slides
node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --no-optional         # HTML without them
        ```

        # Options
        {
            {[.]
                - `-o <path>` -- output file path (default: input name with `.html`, `.pdf` or `.pptx`). With both `--pdf` and `--pptx`, `-o` names the PDF and the PPTX takes the input's name.
                - `--pdf` -- export as PDF via headless Chrome, at the theme's page size
                - `--pptx` (or `--slides`) -- export as PowerPoint. Also runs the layout check, because it has already measured the deck
                - `--check` -- build the HTML, measure every slide in a browser, and report any whose content has run past the margin the theme reserved
                - `--theme <dir>` -- a theme directory holding `theme.css`, and optionally `theme.js`, `theme.json` and assets (default: the built-in theme)
                - `--fit contain|cover|stretch` -- what happens when the window is not the slide's shape: letterbox, crop, or distort (default: `contain`)
                - `--with-optional` / `--no-optional` -- force slides marked `optional: true` in or out, in any output format. By default the HTML build keeps them and the `--pdf` and `--pptx` exports drop them, which is the common case for each; an HTML deck you are sending rather than presenting wants `--no-optional`
                - `--dark` -- dark-mode colour overrides
                - `--help` / `-h` -- show usage information and exit
            }
        }

        `--pdf`, `--pptx` and `--check` all require Google Chrome or Chromium. Set the
        `CHROME_PATH` environment variable to override auto-detection.

        The PowerPoint export has no layout engine of its own: it opens the built deck in
        headless Chrome and asks the browser where every box, rule, image and run of text
        ended up, then turns each into one absolutely positioned shape. A new layout or a
        restyled theme therefore exports correctly without the exporter being taught
        anything. Web fonts are named rather than embedded, and remote images are skipped
        and reported.
    }

    # Notion Sync Tool
    {
        `tools/sync-notion.js` syncs SDOC files to Notion pages. See the [Notion Sync guide](../guide/notion-sync.sdoc) for full setup instructions.

        ```bash
node tools/sync-notion.js <file-or-directory> [options]
        ```

        # Options
        {
            {[.]
                - `--page-id <id>` -- override the Notion page ID (single-file mode only)
                - `--token <tok>` -- Notion integration token (default: `$NOTION_TOKEN` env var)
                - `--dry-run` -- render blocks to stdout without calling the Notion API
                - `--verbose` -- print progress to stderr
                - `--help` / `-h` -- show usage information and exit
            }
        }

        # Examples
        {
            ```bash
# Sync a single file (page ID from @meta)
node tools/sync-notion.js docs/architecture.sdoc --verbose

# Scan a directory for files with notion-page in @meta
node tools/sync-notion.js docs/ --verbose

# Dry run -- inspect the rendered Notion blocks
node tools/sync-notion.js docs/ --dry-run

# Override page ID (for testing)
node tools/sync-notion.js file.sdoc --page-id abc123def456
            ```
        }

        Files opt in to sync by setting `notion-page: <page-id>` in their `@meta` scope. When given a directory, the tool scans recursively for `.sdoc` files with this property.
    }

    # npm Package
    {
        The parser, renderers, and knowledge files are published to npm as `@entropicwarrior/sdoc`:

        ```bash
npm install @entropicwarrior/sdoc
        ```

        See the [API reference](api.sdoc) for available exports.
    }

    # Building the Extension
    {
        ```bash
npm install
npm run package
        ```

        This produces `dist/sdoc-<version>.vsix`. Install it with:

        ```bash
code --install-extension dist/sdoc-<version>.vsix
        ```
    }

    # Document Server
    {
        Two ways to browse all `.sdoc` files in a project with a local HTTP server:

        # Python CLI
        {
            `tools/serve_docs.py` starts a local document server:

            ```bash
python3 tools/serve_docs.py                          # serve current dir on :4070
python3 tools/serve_docs.py docs/ -p 8080            # serve docs/ on :8080
python3 tools/serve_docs.py --no-open                # don't auto-open browser
            ```

            {[.]
                - `source_dir` (positional, default: `.`) -- directory containing `.sdoc` files
                - `-p` / `--port` -- port to serve on (default: `4070`)
                - `--no-open` -- don't open the browser automatically
            }
        }

        # VSCode Command
        {
            Run **SDOC: Browse Documents** from the Command Palette. A prompt asks whether to serve the entire workspace or a specific folder. The server starts on an available port (4070-4079) and opens the viewer in the browser.

            While the server is running, running the command again offers "Open in Browser" or "Stop Server". A status bar item shows the active port.
        }

        # How It Works
        {
            The server serves files directly from the source directory -- no copying, no bundling. The manifest (file list, titles, configs, CSS) is computed fresh on each request, so edits are reflected on browser refresh. Pages render like the live preview, with every signpost shown; only exports drop them.

            {[.]
                - `GET /` -- viewer UI (`src/site-template/index.html`)
                - `GET /viewer.css` -- viewer styles
                - `GET /sdoc-web.js` -- browser-side parser/renderer
                - `GET /api/manifest` -- file list, titles, configs, CSS map
                - `GET /api/content?path=<rel>` -- raw `.sdoc` file content (on demand)
            }
        }

        # Viewer Features
        {
            {[.]
                - Sidebar with file tree navigation and search filtering
                - Split-pane comparison (Shift+Click a second document)
                - Collapsible sidebar with layout controls (single, split L/R, split T/B)
                - Collapsible scopes (toggle triangles on scope headings, same as preview)
                - Client-side rendering with the same parser as the extension
                - Shadow DOM isolation for each document pane
                - URL hash navigation (`#doc=path/to/file.sdoc`)
            }
        }
    }
}
